Consider the weather stations of Fig. 1, each recording one data point: the minimum temperature of a day as feature and its maximum temperature as label. A weather forecasting application uses these data points to learn a hypothesis via empirical risk minimization. For a parametric model with model parameters w and a loss function L(z,w ), the objective function of empirical risk minimization can be written as \[ f({\mathbf w}) :=  rac{1}{m} \sum_{r=1}^{m} L\left({\mathbf z}^{(r)},{\mathbf w} ight) 	ext{.} \] The objective function f is the average loss over the training set D(train) = z(1), …, z(m) of m data points. Note that minimizing f on the training set is not the main goal in machine learning applications. The goal is to learn a hypothesis with a small loss on data points beyond the training set (see generalization). For linear regression, which learns a linear hypothesis under the squared error loss, the above objective function f is differentiable and convex. Gradient descent approaches a minimum of a differentiable function f(w) by repeating the gradient step \[ {\mathbf w}^{(t+1)} = {\mathbf w}^{(t)} - \eta 
abla f({\mathbf w}^{(t)}) 	ext{,} \] with a step size η > 0 that is small enough for each step to decrease f (see gradient descent). The gradient of the above objective function is an average with one term per data point, 
abla f({\mathbf w}) =  rac{1}{m} \sum_{r=1}^{m} 
abla_{{\mathbf w}} L\left({\mathbf z}^{(r)},{\mathbf w} ight). Its exact evaluation therefore requires collecting and processing the entire training set. Collecting the entire training set can be challenging or infeasible when it is large or stored at different locations. SGD approximates the gradient computation by replacing the sum over the entire training set with a sum over a subset of the training set. This subset is chosen randomly and referred to as a batch B ⊆ 1, …, m with batch size B = |B| (see Fig. 1). The resulting gradient approximation is \[ {\mathbf g}^{(\mathcal{B})} ig( {\mathbf w}  ig) :=  rac{1}{B} \sum_{r \in \mathcal{B}} 
abla_{{\mathbf w}} L\left({\mathbf z}^{(r)},{\mathbf w} ight) 	ext{.} \] This gradient approximation can also be interpreted as the approximation of f(w) itself by the average loss over the batch (see Fig. 2). Each SGD iteration generates a new batch B(t) and performs the gradient descent update with g(w) in place of the gradient ∇ f(w). The update reads \[ {\mathbf w}^{(t+1)} = {\mathbf w}^{(t)} - \eta^{(t)} \underbrace{  rac{1}{B} \sum_{r \in \mathcal{B}^{(t)}} 
abla_{{\mathbf w}} L\left({\mathbf z}^{(r)},{\mathbf w}^{(t)} ight)}_{= {\mathbf g}^{(\mathcal{B}^{(t)})} ig( {\mathbf w}^{(t)}  ig)} 	ext{,} \] with a step size η(t) > 0. Conditioned on w(t), the batch B(t) is drawn independently of it, so every data point enters with equal probability. The expectation of the gradient approximation g(B(t))( w(t) ) is therefore the exact gradient ∇ f(w(t)): like any average over uniformly drawn data points, the approximation is an unbiased estimator. The fixed points of the gradient step are the points satisfying the zero-gradient condition ∇ f(w) = 0. One classical set of sufficient conditions for the convergence of such random iterations to a minimum of f is Σt=1∞ η(t) = ∞ and Σt=1∞ (η(t))2 < ∞. The approximation error of the batch gradient is set by the batch size. Given w, the batch gradient is the sample mean of B per-data point gradients drawn uniformly from the training set, so its mean squared error is at most the variance of one draw divided by B,  egin{equation} \mathbb{E}\Big\{  ig\| {\mathbf g}^{(\mathcal{B})} ig( {\mathbf w}  ig) - 
abla f({\mathbf w})  ig\|^{2} \Big| {\mathbf w} \Big\} \le  rac{\sigma^{2}({\mathbf w})}{B} 	ext{,} \quad \sigma^{2}({\mathbf w}) :=  rac{1}{m} \sum_{r=1}^{m}  ig\| 
abla_{{\mathbf w}} L\left({\mathbf z}^{(r)},{\mathbf w} ight) - 
abla f({\mathbf w})  ig\|^{2} 	ext{,} \end{equation} with equality for a batch drawn with replacement. For a β-smooth f, the descent lemma bounds f after one update by its linear approximation at w(t) plus β/2 times the squared length of the update. Taking the conditional expectation given w(t), the linear term produces the exact gradient by unbiasedness and the squared length produces ‖ ∇ f(w(t)) ‖2 plus the error Eq. (1), which gives the progress of one SGD update,  egin{equation} \mathbb{E} ig\{ f({\mathbf w}^{(t+1)})  ig| {\mathbf w}^{(t)}  ig\} \le f({\mathbf w}^{(t)}) - \eta^{(t)} \Big( 1 -  rac{ eta \eta^{(t)}}{2} \Big) \Vert {
abla f({\mathbf w}^{(t)})} \Vert^{2} +  rac{ eta  ig(\eta^{(t)} ig)^{2}}{2}  rac{\sigma^{2}({\mathbf w}^{(t)})}{B} 	ext{.} \end{equation} The first two terms of Eq. (2) are the progress of a gradient step with the exact gradient; the last term is the price of the gradient approximation, which shrinks with the batch size through Eq. (1) and with the step size through its square. The two step size conditions above make that price summable over the iterations while the progress is not. The batch size B is the parameter of SGD that trades the computational cost of its iteration against the approximation error incurred in g(B)( w ) ≈ ∇ f(w). One extreme case is B = m, i.e., the batch contains the entire training set and the gradient approximation becomes exact: g(B)( w ) = ∇ f(w). At B = 1, it approximates the gradient of f using a single data point, resulting in a significant approximation error (see Fig. 3). SGD with 1 < B < m is referred to as mini-batch SGD. Each update costs B gradient evaluations ∇ L(z(r),w(t) ), for r ∈ B, instead of m evaluations required by computing the exact gradient. However, the update introduces an approximation error which decreases as the batch size grows (see Fig. 3). Algorithm 1 states the method in full.  egin{algorithm}[H] \caption{SGD }  egin{algorithmic}[1] \Statex Input: training set D(train), loss function, batch size B, iteration count T, step sizes η(t) > 0 for t = 0, …, T-1, initialization w(0) \For{t = 0, 1, …, T-1} \State draw a batch B(t) of B indices uniformly at random \State compute g(B(t))( w(t) ) \State w(t+1) := w(t) - η(t) g(B(t))( w(t) ) \EndFor \Statex Output: w(T) \end{algorithmic} \end{algorithm} Step 3 of Algorithm 1 evaluates B gradients of the loss function, each of them d numbers for a linear model, so one iteration costs on the order of B d arithmetic operations against m d for an exact gradient. That lower computational cost of SGD allows the training of high-dimensional models from large decentralized collections of data points. A prime example of a machine learning application that involves large decentralized data is weather forecasting: the stations of Fig. 1, which open this entry, each hold a single data point, and a batch is a handful of stations polled for one gradient step.
